Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_AHEAD_GUARD (+ _SETS, _MAX_MS) and EDEN_UP_AHEAD_ALWAYS: run a frame ahead (EDEN_NX2_FRAMES_AHEAD=1), a guest thread that writes a buffer page the frame in flight may still read waits for that frame's fence; off by default

Run a frame ahead, Ocarina rewrites buffer memory for a later frame while the GPU thread still
draws the presented one, and the GPU thread reads guest memory when it draws.
EDEN_NX2_OVERWRITE_CHECK on T86 (run 736, v2.4-fast): about 9 of 10 frames had such reads (~700
collected writes and 10-70 reads a frame); the fade to black showed every second frame drawn with
the value of two frames later, the frames whose present returned at once.

The guard notes the device pages the buffer cache reads from guest memory (uploads and streamed
uniform blocks, at the EDEN_NX2_OVERWRITE_CHECK read sites), frame by frame; a frame ends at its
present's fence. When a present returns before its fence, the pages read in the last finished
frames (EDEN_UP_AHEAD_GUARD_SETS, default one and two presents back) and in this frame so far get
+1 on their cached count, so every guest write to them goes through HandleRasterizerWrite (the
PS5 has no fastmem arena: a page table flip, no mprotect), and a guest thread on a CPU core that
writes one waits there until the GPU thread reaches the fence. A wait ends after
EDEN_UP_AHEAD_GUARD_MAX_MS (500), and with EDEN_UP_AHEAD_GUARD_GPU_ESCAPE=1 after 3 ms once a GPU
channel is seen suspended; either lifts the guard for the frame. One log line per 300 presents: arms, pages, how many writes waited and how long, by core
and by guest thread id.

EDEN_UP_AHEAD_ALWAYS=1 (after GrowingBlackBeans' 0911): no second wait when the previous frame
was still on the GPU at the present. For tests with the guard.

Not covered: texture data and descriptor tables the texture cache reads.

---
